A||||50||JetroGT||Vortec 4.3L Swap?
Z||000000||JetroGT||07-30-99||08:26 PM||||Has anyone ever thought of putting a Vortec 4.3L into a Fiero using one of the V8 install kits. The 4.3 is just a 350 with two of the cyl. choped off. I think it would fit better too. Plus the fact that GM makes a turbo for it. I haven't seen any one put that engine in a Fiero. Is their any reason why it won't work?
Z||000001||batboy||07-30-99||08:47 PM||||I have wondered the same thing. Always thought that would be a great swap. Lots of high performance parts for the 4.3 engine. Once while surfing the 'net, I came across one of the Fiero club's web site (don't remember which one) where they mentioned that a member had done one of those swaps and liked it. There were not many details about it though, if I remember correctly. Question: is the Vortec 4.3 the same as the older 4.3 engines. The older ones could bolt up to any bellhousing or tranny that a Chevy small block V8 could.
Z||000002||JetroGT||07-30-99||08:59 PM||||The Block is the same. It is basicly the same except for the intake and the fuel injection. A friend of mine has a Sonoma GT with that engine. It pulls that truck along very nicely. It got me thinking how it would push my little Fiero without all that extra weight to hold it back.
Z||000003||lowCG||07-30-99||11:02 PM||||Always wondered why not that motor over the 3800,way more potential!<br>Could probably actually find a tunnel ram,aluminum heads,roller cam for it too.
Z||000004||four50four||07-31-99||01:38 PM||||what do you think the weight difference would be over a 2.8 v6?
Z||000005||Eric||07-31-99||01:56 PM||||Shouldn't weigh that much more.  I can tell you first hand that the 2.8 is a very heavy small disp. V6.  I think the 2.8 long block weighs 350 lbs.  If it had aluminum heads, it would shed about 40-50 lbs, but you can't easily put them on that engine. <p>The 4.3 motor is already a screamer, even on the heavier "lightweight" trucks.  I think it has a stock rating of 190hp and 250 ft/lbs of torque.  
Z||000006||squid||08-02-99||09:12 PM||||I have a 4.3 and I've purchased a partial v8 archie kit to use. I've collected all my parts except tubing and hoses for coolant. The '85 4.3's had the large crank flange like the older small block chevys and only need a hub adapter to use the 2.8l neutral balanced flywheel. The later motors have to use a custom flywheel only Archie makes. I think its $300. After I finish my trucks I'll start on this. 
Z||000007||batboy||08-02-99||10:34 PM||||My dad has an older 4.3 laying around in his shed that he said I could have (needs rebuilt though). Any idea how much it will cost for this swap if a guy did most of his own work. Maybe just bought the basic swap kit, like the bellhousing adapter, etc. Sure wish there was a front wheel drive tranny that the 4.3 would bolt up to. That would be nice. Squid, keep us posted on your progress. I like the 4.3 idea, it's a great engine, maybe about the best V6 made.
Z||000008||squid||08-03-99||04:54 PM||||My parts from Archie were $610. All I got was the crank hub adapter, block to trans adapter,and the "V" passengerside support. I wont need a special water pump setup or cut a hole on the passengerside and I should be able to get by with a stock v6 radiator. I got a neutral flywheel from the junkyard for $30 and a core motor '85 4.3 with carbed hi-rise intake (astro van)and exhaust manifolds for $100(I pieced it together from several cores laying around). I've had it machined and I got all my rebuild parts from P.A.W. including aluminum roller rockers($150) and a reduction starter from Jegs($150?).Kit car did a build up awhile back of the carbed 4.3. They tried different intakes, heads,headers, and cams but the biggest gain was from a cam(280 HP I think and still good torque). The headers and intake already flow good. I just removed the casting flash and port matched.  
Z||000009||lowCG||08-03-99||06:28 PM||||Those Astro Vans really boogie,had a friend and I very surprised when one of us stepped on it in this borrowed van,could hardly believe it was stock.Just looked at each other with puzzled gaze<br>You should post the dimensions of the(machined)adapting components for those who have shop facilities :)<br>Are you using a roller cam?<br>How big are the valves?
Z||000010||spike||08-05-99||10:38 AM||||This swap sounds interesting. Is the 4.3 a really good engine like everyone says. My Father in law has his own CNC Machine Shop and<br>I am sure he would be able to fabricate the<br>Tranny adaptor plate for much less then the purchase cost from Archie. Only problem is I would need a mock-up maybe made from a piece of Plywood shaped to match the original piece from Archie. I guess Archie would not like this too much but thats life. If I can save some bucks and invest in a better engine and installation why not. You mentioned a Crank Hub Adapter. Is this hub adaptor needed for all 4.3 engines or just a certain year. If someone can provide a plywood template of Archies tranny adaptor we can all save bucks and have a 4.3 or V8 in our Fieros for next to nothing. Wouldn't it be great.
Z||000011||squid||08-05-99||04:32 PM||||The hub adapter is needed on '85 4.3s only that are internally balanced or early small block chevys that are internally balanced.It bolts to the end of the crank and is the same diameter as the crank flange(3.58"?)and is the same thickness as the block to transmission adapter(about an inch). Then a neutral balanced flywheel for a 3.1 or internally balanced 2.8 is bolted on to the adapter. The adapter also supports the pilot shaft of the transmission.If you use a newer external balance 4.3 or v8 with the one piece crank seal then you will need a custom made flywheel that looks like a 3.1/2.8 flywheel but is drilled and balanced for a 4.3/v8. I think Archies prices for the individual pieces are not much more than what it would cost in materials alone (just the block adapter weighs about 50 pounds) and I wont cheat him. Hes already been ripped off by several people.
Z||000012||lowCG||08-05-99||08:02 PM||||Wow,that's pretty heavy,is the adaptor plate made out of steel?<br>Have made them before out of aluminum(3/8")plate.<br>
Z||000013||batboy||08-05-99||08:56 PM||||My V8 adapter plate appears to be a massive 1.5 inch thick steel plate. Remember, this has to withstand the torque of a small block engine. I don't know how much it weighs though. I've thought about making a template of it if I ever have to drop the cradle, not to sell or try to rip anybody off, but just for personal use.
Z||000014||lowCG||08-06-99||12:02 AM||||That's big enough for a Pratt$Whitney gas turbine!Must be a needed depth for counter bores(bolts into bellhousing) or something.Low grade,cast aluminum ears on most bellhousings are much weaker than aircraft tooling scraps I use,or most alloys for that matter.<br>Two digital drawings put together,then printed out on a decent plotter will produce an adequate template also.
Z||000015||squid||08-06-99||08:42 PM||||Since there are only 2 major v8 fiero builders, Batboy and I probably have the same kit.The block adapter is steel and heavy. The starter bolts to it too. It will probably outlast my whole car even if I totalled it. My biggest problem will be the passengerside engine support. On a v8(4 inches longer) thew support ends up being right over the frame rail. I'm going to have to weld on some box tubing to extend the frame to under the front of the block. Batboy, do you use the in-tank fuel pump with a regulator? Does it shorten the life of the pump by not having a return line so it runs cooler?
Z||000016||batboy||08-08-99||03:26 PM||||My fuel pump is a standard in-line Holley electric mounted close to the fuel tank. Seems to work pretty good. This was already done before I bought the car.
Z||000017||batboy||08-18-99||08:29 AM||||Squid, don't forget to keep us posted on your 4.3 conversion progress. I've found out that my Dad's extra engine is a 1988 4.3 liter. So this means I'd have to use the custom built flywheel for a Fiero swap, right? What about using an auto tranny? Probably still need a custom flexplate, right?
Z||000018||batboy||08-18-99||08:29 AM||||Squid, don't forget to keep us posted on your 4.3 conversion progress. I've found out that my Dad's extra engine is a 1988 4.3 liter. So this means I'd have to use the custom built flywheel for a Fiero swap, right? What about using an auto tranny? Probably still need a custom flexplate, right?
Z||000019||batboy||08-18-99||10:54 AM||||Oh no, my first double post! Oops!<p>Hey, another 4.3 question. I've found out that there were a few diesel 4.3 V6/90 degree engines put into some front wheel drive Olds Cutlass Cieras, Pontiac 6000's, and Chevy Celebrities. Does anyone know if the gasoline 4.3 engines have the same bellhousing bolt pattern as the diesel? I'm thinking if they are both the same, a guy could find one of those FWD trannies in order to eliminate the need for a conversion adapter plate.
Z||000020||squid||08-18-99||04:59 PM||||I havent even started on it yet. Its the only car with working air conditioning right now and I havent finished my 351 '83 ranger.Your '88 is externally balanced and has the one piece rear main seal.You will need a flywheel or flexplate that is balanced to fit your engine yet is small enough to fit in a fiero bellhousing. To keep from using a custom flywheel and to be able to use Archie's hub adapter with a stock neutral balanced flywheel or flexplate from a 3.1, you would have to have your engine internally balanced and I assume you dont want to rebuild your dads engine yet.Another way is to use a '85 crank and oil pan with a special block adapter that they make for v8s to use early cranks in late blocks and the adapter has to be indexed with the crank since it holds your oil seal(sounds like a pain). It would be alot easier for you to use the custom flywheel from Archie.You've also got a roller cam. Mine is a flat tappet and I cant use the fatory roller stuff because I have shorter lifter bores. About the diesels, I've never seen or heard of those but even if you found one of those automatics they would probably be really expensive to rebuild if they were rare. Let me know if you find one.   
Z||000021||spike||08-19-99||07:29 AM||||Bat, I have also seen this somewhere on the web while surfing. I tried to find it but had no luck. I will look again today. 
Z||000022||VISCERAL||08-19-99||08:39 AM||||4.3 adapter plate, mounts, brackets for fiero. Check it out<p>http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/
Z||000023||mwbackus||08-19-99||06:58 PM||||Isn't that a billet aluminum adapter plate on their web site?  That would weigh alot less, anyone know how much they want for one?  This is a very interesting thread!  Im even starting to rethink my plans for a 3.4 swap.<p>Mike
Z||000024||squid||08-19-99||10:11 PM||||I found this guy on the fiero web ring that installed a 3.4 DOHC in his '88. He has alot of pics.He also has a pic of him trying to replace the forward spark plugs. You cant do it without lowering the cradle. He said everything bolts to the cradle unmodified. That cnc milled adapter looks like it uses a Ford starter. Definately alot cheaper than using a gear reduction (required with Archies). 
Z||000025||lowCG||08-20-99||12:52 AM||||Good thing you don't need to change the plugs as often as with the dinasaur motors,huh?<br>Did you see the sizes of the intake/exhaust of that 4cam motor? (they're huge!)<br>Only 9.25:1 compression on these too,which is pretty low for a 4 valve engine;just think of the gains from some nice headwork,and some forged 11:1 pistons.<br>Just need to get one for now though.<br>Still think the 4.3 should've come stock in the Fiero.
Z||000026||VISCERAL||08-20-99||01:10 PM||||From my earlier post that guy makes flywheels for 259 out of steel, bellhousing adapters for 149 aluminum and 249 steel. all the mounts and brackets for 175 but you would have to drill them yourself though. I think I willgo the 4.3 route instead of all the other v6's. Because of the cubes, which you can't beat. You'll have great torque, parts accessibility from chevy v8's, roller cam, maybe aluminum heads, then one could either throw on a carb, or various fuel injection units:edelbrock-holley-etc...<br>Then throw on a turbo or two and maybe an intercooler. w/o turbo 250-300 horses w/turbo????. Sounds like enough for me. Plus no chassis cuts like archies. less weight than v8 and more practicality than a v8 with the weight of a v6.    Patrick Szewczyk
Z||000027||mwbackus||08-20-99||08:30 PM||||Hmm.. I wonder if one of those turbo intercooled 4.3's out of a Syclone would drop in?  280 HP @ 4200 and 360 ft/lb @ 3600, stock.. <p>http://www.syty.org/brochure-spec.html<p>Sounds pretty good..hehe<p>Mike
Z||000028||squid||08-20-99||10:38 PM||||Since my original ac compressor lines are leaking,they are a "dealer only" item, and I need a v belt compressor clutch, I going to use a Vintage Air street rod type universal compressor with mount brackets(attach to the fuel pump block off plate bolts) and lines for @$300. Then I can use a low mount alternator bracket in front. To use Archies ac bracket, you have to use a special harmonic balancer/pulley. I've got the CVR starter from Jegs. Also, the oil filter bypass for a 4.3 is not the same as a small block chevy. 4.3's use a metric thread. For the most clearance, Jegs has one with 90 degree fittings instead of coming straight out. Batboy, I saw your site. Those cast iron manifolds arent pretty but I'm sure they help keep it alittle cooler back there than if you had regular noncoated headers. What a sleeper! Is it loud? Do you have cats? How is your exhaust run? Do you have rubber hose all the way from the engine to the side water pipes or do you have some tubing in between mounted to the cradle or firewall?
Z||000029||batboy||08-23-99||01:33 AM||||Squid, 1) Next spring, I hope to install headers (I've seen those block hugging shorty streetrod headers that might work). At the same time, I'm going to change the heads over to Dart Aluminum high performance heads (should be over 40 lbs lighter). I've wondered about the heat thing, maybe I'll have to wrap the headers with that heat insulation stuff. 2) Yes it's loud. A bit too loud in my opinion. 3) No cats. 4) It's a dual exhaust through glass packs. I should take a picture next time I borrow my buddies digital camera. 5) The main engine radiator hoses consist of four 12 inch heavy duty wire reinforced rubber hoses with an appropriate length of metal tube in the middle. These are not mounted, probably too much vibration and engine flex to mount them solid.
Z||000030||batboy||08-23-99||01:40 AM||||If and when I do the heads and headers on the Batmobile, I'd like to change over to Flowmaster mufflers rather than the glass packs it came with. In the mean time, if I were to install a pair of resonated exhaust tips, would this mellow out the sound and maybe quiet it down a little? I've almost decided to order some from JEGS, they sure look good too. Any opinions from anyone?
Z||000031||chris6878||08-23-99||07:38 AM||||Hey Guys.<p>I am getting ready to purchase an 85 GT Fiero. It Has a 2.8 engine in it with an automatic transmission. The engine is KNOCKING like crazy though... I can purchase the car for $500. Body is in great shape, needs better paint job though. It has air, trunk release, sunroof, ect... pretty nice car..  I offered the lady $400 for the car but she refuses to go any lower than 500... so i will probally purchase the car anyway in the next day or two..<p>I am strongly interested in the 4.3 V6 conversion. This car is going to need motor work anyway..  my as well install something that can handle my lead foot...  <p>Whats is an estimate on the cost for the conversion..? and what parts would be needed just to get the engine in the car properly? I was thinking of and V8 from Archie's but... that a little too much for my budget.. i know where i can get a 4.3 engine at anyway.. probally cheap..<p>My daily driver now is a 84 model... Its a great car.. just not enough horses... And besides the 85 GT has better looks than mine..<p>I will glady apprieciate any info...  Thanks Guys..<p>Chris
Z||000032||Phil||08-23-99||07:56 PM||||I see all this talk about monster HP and touque numbers-- be advised that the weak link  in the drive train will be the tranny. Fiero V6 4 and 5spds don't like 250+ ft-lbs of twisting. Just my $0.02 worth
Z||000033||squid||08-23-99||08:46 PM||||Batboy, the stainless steel superttraps will let you adjust the noise level somewhat. Resonator tips usually dont do a whole lot. I agree, they do look good though. Exhaust wrap  causes mild steel headers to rust out sooner. Ceramic coat huggers are great but the cheapest I've seen are $185. Phil, I agree about the trannys but how heavy the car is has a huge affect on what they can handle. By the way everybody, Smoky Yunick built a full race 4.3 with 2, 2 barrels that put out 437 hp. Kit car did a build up with the stock '85 astro shortblock, chevy bowtie heads and a chevy bowtie crossram intake with a single 650 cfm 4 barrel that put out 280 ft lbs@4800rpm and 275 hp at 5600 rpm.Not too bad!
Z||000034||mwbackus||08-23-99||09:17 PM||||Phil, if the Fiero trans holds up to a high performance V8, it should handle a built 4.3 just fine.  <br>    The manifolds/crosssover on the turbo 4.3 Sy/Ty seem to be engineered in such a way that they may work without extensive modification.  Any input regarding this would be appreciated.<br>  <br>Some stupid questions :  What is the large pulley directly below the alternator?  And how hard will it be to relocate the alternator on the turbo motor?<p>Mike
