Z||000048||JimiZ||08-25-99||06:52 PM||||You know, all this talk about 4.3L conversions and such has got me all to thinking.  The only Fiero I ever had was a 1984 2m4. I didn't much like that one.  But a Fiero with a Vortech 4.3L with swirl heads would be a treat!  My friend Rick has a bunch of mods for his Fiero that he's just about ready to do.  Think we'll wait 'till it cools off before doing them though.  If all goes well, I just might have to get one of my own to play with.  For now, I'll just play with his Fiero and let him pay for it...hahaha.
Z||000049||batboy||08-25-99||08:52 PM||||The gasoline 4.3 V6/90 degree engine that we are referring to is a Chevy design. It truly is a Chevy 350 V8 missing 2 cylinders. It has the same bellhousing bolt pattern, same stroke, and same cylinder diameter as a small block 350. Lots of V8 parts interchange with the 4.3 engine.<p>This is one good, strong V6 with a proven high performance track record. I was hoping to find a FWD car tranny that the 4.3 would fit onto, but looks like the only way to install one into a Fiero is to use the basic Chevy V8 conversion kit and go from there.
Z||000050||mshill||08-27-99||12:31 PM||||I love the Chevy 4.3 V6.<p>I have one in my GMC Safari van with MPFI an it has 100K on it and I pull my 18ft OB Boat frequently.  The engines are strong and solid.<p>I also have the same engine in my boat. It started as a 175 2bbl and I did the following: 1) 1.6:1 roller rockers, 2) Edelbrock Performance Manifold 3) Holley Pro-Jection throttle body fuel injection.  I now have 225 horses and the economy of a 2bbl carb setup still.  It is as good as many small block setup in most boats sold.<br>
